### How does parallelSort work?

Arrays.parallelSort uses a parallel merge sort. It splits the array into chunks, sorts each chunk on a separate thread from the common ForkJoinPool, and merges the sorted chunks.

```java
package com.bank.parallel;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class ParallelSortDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] amounts = new BigDecimal[1_000_000];
        for (int i = 0; i < amounts.length; i++) {
            amounts[i] = BigDecimal.valueOf(Math.random() * 100000);
        }
        Arrays.parallelSort(amounts);  // parallel merge sort on common ForkJoinPool
        System.out.println("Smallest: " + amounts[0]);
        System.out.println("Largest: " + amounts[amounts.length - 1]);
    }
}
```

Internally it goes parallel above approximately 8192 elements. Below that threshold, it calls sequential `Arrays.sort`, which uses a dual-pivot quicksort for primitives and TimSort for objects.

### How does parallelPrefix work?

parallelPrefix computes a running cumulative operation across the array. After the call, each element at index i contains the cumulative result of applying the operator from index 0 through i.

```java
package com.bank.parallel;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class ParallelPrefixDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] dailyNet = {
            new BigDecimal("10000"),
            new BigDecimal("-2500"),
            new BigDecimal("5000"),
            new BigDecimal("-1000"),
            new BigDecimal("3000")
        };
        Arrays.parallelPrefix(dailyNet, BigDecimal::add);
        // dailyNet is now: [10000, 7500, 12500, 11500, 14500]
        // dailyNet[i] is the cumulative balance through day i
        System.out.println("Day 5 cumulative balance: " + dailyNet[4]);
    }
}
```

### How does parallelSetAll work?

parallelSetAll fills each element from its index using a generator function, computed in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.parallel;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class ParallelSetAllDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] fees = new BigDecimal[1000];
        BigDecimal baseFee = new BigDecimal("10.00");
        BigDecimal step = new BigDecimal("0.50");
        Arrays.parallelSetAll(fees,
            i -> baseFee.add(step.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(i))));
        // fees[0] = 10.00, fees[1] = 10.50, fees[999] = 509.50
        System.out.println("Fee at index 999: " + fees[999]);
    }
}
```

### What is the common ForkJoinPool concern?

All three methods share the common pool with parallel streams and default CompletableFutures. A long parallel sort on a million elements can delay other parallel work in the same JVM process. Do not mix blocking IO into anything sharing the common pool.

### When does parallel actually pay off?

| Condition | Parallel Helps? | Reason |
|-----------|----------------|--------|
| Large array (10K+ elements) | Yes | Enough work to amortize overhead |
| Multi-core machine (4+ cores) | Yes | Actual parallelism available |
| Non-trivial per-element cost | Yes | More work per element means more benefit |
| Small array (less than 1K) | No | Overhead dominates actual work |
| Single-core machine | No | No parallelism, only overhead |
| Cheap comparison (int sort) | Maybe not | Comparison cost too low to offset fork/join overhead |

### How do you benchmark parallel vs sequential?

Use JMH to measure accurately. JVM warm-up, JIT compilation, and garbage collection can mislead naive benchmarks.

```java
@Benchmark
public void sequentialSort(Blackhole bh) {
    BigDecimal[] copy = amounts.clone();
    Arrays.sort(copy);
    bh.consume(copy);
}

@Benchmark
public void parallelSort(Blackhole bh) {
    BigDecimal[] copy = amounts.clone();
    Arrays.parallelSort(copy);
    bh.consume(copy);
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Using parallelSort on small arrays adds overhead without benefit. Another mistake is assuming parallelPrefix produces the same result as a sequential loop when the operator is not associative. The operator must be associative (order of grouping does not matter) for parallel prefix to produce correct results.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you change the common ForkJoinPool parallelism? Set the system property `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N`.
- Can you run parallelSort on a custom ForkJoinPool? Not directly. The API always uses the common pool. You can submit the entire sort as a task to a custom pool.
- How does parallelPrefix implement the parallel scan algorithm? It uses a two-pass approach: an up-sweep (reduce) to compute subtotals, then a down-sweep to propagate the cumulative result.
